{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518502432,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE0NzA5NzM4MjY="},"added":["metadata"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519063757,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2Njc2ODAzOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2144#issuecomment-366768039"},"message":"After this has come up twice I thought about it for a moment, and I came to the conclusion the that present behavior is OK.\n\nPresent behavior: `datalad.metadata.maxfieldsize` takes a size in bytes/chars (default 100k), and based on that any metadata field that exceeds this size is rejected/excluded. Importantly, for nested metadata structures this will consider all information in a nested structure combined. A concrete example is the output of the `dicom` extractor that include a summary of unique DICOM properties of all images in all image series in a dataset. With ~10 different image series we might hit that threshold.\n\nConclusion: I think this is a useful limit to have. It likely should be amended (but not replaced by) a per \"bottom-level\" field limit. In cases like the example above the situation can be rectified by specifying a different limit in a dataset's configuration.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1519063757,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTQ4MTA3MDYxMA=="},"status":2}]}